<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Padua</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Patavium, Padova</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Padua (Padova) is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. As Patavium it was a leading city of the ancient Veneti and a wealthy Roman municipium, the home of the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>. Its university, founded in 1222, is one of the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the city came under Venetian rule in 1405, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>'s frescoes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scrovegni-chapel">Scrovegni Chapel</a> are among its most famous monuments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/padua.jpg" alt="Padua" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hannelore · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prato_della_Valle.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Padua lies on the banks of the Bacchiglione, 40 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and 29 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>, in the Venetian Plain. The Brenta River, which once ran through the city, still touches its northern districts, and the Euganaean Hills to the south-west appear in poems by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-bysshe-shelley">Percy Bysshe Shelley</a>. The origin of the Roman name Patavium is uncertain; it may be connected with Padus, the ancient name of the Po, or with a Gaulish word for pine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to a tradition at least as old as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, the Trojan prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antenor">Antenor</a> founded Padua around 1183 BCE, leading the Eneti or Veneti to the Euganean plain. When a stone sarcophagus was dug up in 1274, officials of the medieval commune declared its remains to be Antenor's. Attila sacked the city savagely in 450. It then fell to the Gothic kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> and Theodoric, was reconquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 540 and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> in 568. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> sacked it in 899.</p><p>In the early eleventh century the citizens established a constitution with a general council and an executive body, and in 1138 the government was entrusted to two consuls. Jacopo da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> was elected lord of Padua in 1318, and nine members of the Carrara family ruled until 1405, apart from short periods under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaligeri</a> and Giangaleazzo Visconti. Padua came under the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1405 and stayed under it until 1797, except for a few weeks in 1509 during the wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-cambrai">League of Cambrai</a>.</p><p>After 1797 the city passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1806 and, from 1814, to Lombardy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. It was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1866. Padua was the Italian army's main command in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the armistice with Austria was signed at Villa Giusti in Padua on 3 November 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Padua is the capital of its province, with 208,202 inhabitants. It has two UNESCO World Heritage entries, its Botanical Garden, the world's oldest, and its fourteenth-century frescoes, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>'s cycle of 1305 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scrovegni-chapel">Scrovegni Chapel</a>. The University of Padua, founded in 1222, is one of the oldest in the world; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a> observed Jupiter's moons from Padua in 1610, and in 1678 Elena Lucrezia Cornaro Piscopia became the first woman in the world to graduate from a university.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Padua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He had already taken Verona and Vicenza in 1387 and <b>Padua</b> in 1388; he then acquired Pisa in 1399, received the submission of Siena and Perugia in 1399–1400 and took Bologna in 1402.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The mainland Terraferma, conquered between 1339 and 1428, including Treviso, <b>Padua</b>, Vicenza, Verona, Brescia and Bergamo, kept its own statutes and local councils under Venetian rectors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Florence, Venice, <b>Padua</b>, Milan and dozens of smaller communes and lordships needed men who could write Latin for contracts, letters, laws and speeches, and the notaries, lawyers and chancery secretaries who did so formed a large lay educated class.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In the same year Andreas Vesalius, professor at <b>Padua</b>, published On the Fabric of the Human Body, whose detailed woodcuts, based on his own dissections, corrected many of Galen’s errors.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>By a decree of Pope Boniface VIII, the Sapienza was founded in Rome as a studium for ecclesiastical studies, under more control than the free-standing universities of Bologna and <b>Padua</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-20">20 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic rewarded him with a lifetime post at <b>Padua</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
